  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 137 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nNortherly to northeasterly winds remain elevated around 15 mph, \ngusting up to 25 mph across much of the region with strongest winds \nto the west over the higher terrain behind the frontal passage \novernight Thursday. High temperatures are expected to remain in the \nmid 70s, with a potential for upper 60s in the higher terrain of \nnorthern Lea County and low 80s along the Rio Grande. PoPS are \nexpected to increase to 30% - 40% spanning from the Guadalupes into \nthe Lower Trans Pecos and in the northeast Permian Basin in the more \nhumid air behind the cold front. Winds are expected to remain \nnortherly to northeasterly allowing for overnight lows to drop to \nthe 50s across much of the region with 40s in the higher terrain to \nthe west and 60s along the river basins. Similar conditions are \nexpected on Saturday, with slightly higher PoPs. \n\nMid to upper level ridging redeveloping to the west from late \nweekend to early next week will allows for a moderation in \ntemperatures Sunday through early next week. Overnight lows are on \nSaturday are expected to remain similar to Friday night. \nTemperatures early next week are expected to slowly return to the \nmid 80s across much of the forecast area, with a potential for low \n90s along the river basins, aided by returning southeasterly winds. \nHowever, as a result of the surface and mid level ridging offset \nfrom the forecast area, the risk for daily showers/storms remain \neach afternoon/evening, but timing, coverage, and rainfall amounts \nin any storms late weekend through early next week are uncertain at \nthis time.\n\n-Bynum",
